Patch: transitionInterval
Letter: F
Requires: B (offsetScan), E (zoneNameScan)
Files: src/impl/util.js, src/zones/IANAZone.js
Summary: one transition-free interval cache, shared by the offset and the zone-name lookup

F: both Intl lookups re-derive the same answer for every value

Requires B and E, whose scanners it anchors on. An offset or a name only changes
at a transition, so a value inside a span already known to be transition-free
needs no Intl call at all. That is one idea and it applies to both of the calls
B and E just made cheap, so this is one cache used twice rather than the same
cache written twice: impl/util.js grows a small interval memo, and
IANAZone#offset and parseZoneInfo each hand it their own lookup.

The interval is exact, not a heuristic. Probes are spaced 2 days apart, and the
shortest window in which any zone's offset changes and returns to where it
started is 6.96 days (America/Boa_Vista, Oct 2000; measured over all 68214
transitions the runtime's own ICU reports, via easy-tz's
tools/tz-transition-gap.ts). Departing and returning inside a probe pair is the
only way two agreeing probes could be wrong, so a shorter window proves the span
transition-free.

Sharing one cache between the two means the bound has to hold for the stricter
of them, because a name can move while the offset stays put —
America/Cambridge_Bay spent the 2000 Nunavut experiment doing exactly that. It
does hold, and it is the same number: the signature bound (CLDR name + offset)
is 6.96 days over the same window, so the 2-day spacing keeps its 3.5x margin
either way. That tool asserts the spacing against a fresh measurement of the
runtime's own ICU, so tzdata tightening past it fails loudly rather than
quietly serving a stale name.

Two spans per zone rather than one, each anchored around the instant that missed
rather than extended in whichever direction the last miss travelled. Both of
those are about the CALLER, not the data, and one span extended one way was wrong
on both counts. Placing a local time asks the zone about three instants, because
fixOffset cannot turn a local time into an instant without an offset and cannot
get the offset without an instant: it seeds itself with the offset at
Settings.now(), then probes twice around the target. So a single span is evicted
by the next lookup of the same parse, every parse, and the direction the last
miss travelled belongs to whichever of the three sites it was.

What makes that permanent rather than a warm-up is a deadlock. A span starts as a
single point, since reach is 0 and only grows after a hit, and a point can never
be hit. One call site escapes anyway, because with nothing else evicting it a
widened span survives long enough to be used — which is why reading strings that
carry their own offset, and so never call the seed, hit 99% of the time. Three
call sites never escape: the widened span is gone before anything can hit it, so
hits stays 0 and reach stays 0. The distance between the instants has nothing to
do with it. Reading today's dates missed just as reliably as reading dates three
years out, 2.13 Intl reads per parse either way, and two anchored spans take both
to zero.

The interleaved order in benchmarks/test/offset-patches.test.ts is that pattern,
asserted by counting rather than by comparing, since a cache that never hits is
still correct and no parity check would notice.

Two is the structural minimum — one span for the seed, one for the target, whose
probes sit about an hour apart and share a span unless a transition falls between
them. A third span for that case measured as nothing on either a day of minutes
near today or a day a year out, and as 0.24 reads per parse against 0.59 on a
year read at 6-hour spacing, which was not worth its branches.

How wide to fan out is still decided by what the previous span paid back, so a
sequential reader widens toward the cap while a scattered one stays near the
floor. The floor is one probe each way and cannot be zero, for the same reason
the deadlock exists: a span that reaches nothing can never be hit, and a budget
that only grows after a hit could then never grow. The pair shares the budget, so
widening costs what it did before.

Worth saying that this treats the symptom. The seed lookup is the whole reason
there are three sites, and it exists only because fixOffset needs somewhere to
start; probing at the target first would remove it, the interleaving with it, and
the need for a second span. That is not a cache change, though. Stock declares a
hole whenever its two probes disagree, which is sound only because the seed is
already close, so a target-first fixOffset needs a third probe before it may
conclude the same — a change to how luxon resolves ambiguous local times rather
than a behavior-preserving one, and out of scope for a patch set that does not
move semantics. Someone upstream could take it.

The two callers differ only in where the state lives, and that difference is
what decides how each is reset. IANAZone keeps a map keyed by zone, cleared by
its resetCache() alongside the others. The name side hangs the interval off the
scanner that E already caches per locale and style, so Locale.resetCache() drops
it with the scanner and no new hook is needed.

One divergence from stock, and it is stock that is strange. Asked for a GENERIC
name (shortGeneric/longGeneric), ICU answers America/Cambridge_Bay with "MT"
everywhere except the one repeated hour of a fall-back transition, where it
returns "MT (Cambridge Bay)" -- an instant-dependent answer for a name whose
whole point is not to depend on the instant. A span brackets that hour and
serves the neighbouring answer. Over 218,100 comparisons against stock across 16
zones, 5 locales, all 6 styles and 4 access orders, that accounts for every one
of the 258 differences, and there are none in the offset or in short/long, which
are the only two styles luxon's own tokens ask for.

